  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "### Findings (2026-09-29): companion frame, $\\langle0|=e_8^T\\otimes e_8^T$\n\n$\\dim W_d$ / $\\dim(W_d\\cap V_1)$ for all 14 generators is $d=0$: 1/1, $d=1$: 7/2, $d=2$: 24/3, $d=3$: 48/3, $d=4$: 64/8.\n\n- **Structured localized states.** There are exactly three, all pure monomials, each localized on its own. Checked exactly at points 1 and 2.\n  - $1\\to e_8$, weight (0,1,2).\n  - $t_{11}(\\theta_1)\\to e_5$, weight (1,1,1).\n  - $t_{10}(\\theta_1+h)\\,t_{11}(\\theta_1)\\to$ a combination of $e_3$, weight (1,2,0), and $e_8$.\n  \n  $t_{11}(\\theta_1)^2$ is **not** localized. With only $r=0$ you get just $1$ and $t_{11}(\\theta_1)$ at every degree. Any $S\\ni 0,1$ (or $0,r$) gives the third state at degree 2.\n- **No singlet below cyclicity.** No structured state has an $e_1$ component, so none reaches the singlet. The localized dimension stays at 3 until $W_d$ is (almost) all of $\\mathbb C^{64}$. Then it jumps to 8, including the singlet direction. Examples:\n  - all points: degree 4;\n  - $\\{0,1\\}$: degree 5;\n  - $\\{0,3\\}$: 6 localized at degree 4, where $\\dim W=61$.\n\n  This is the generic bound $\\dim(W\\cap V_1)\\ge\\dim W-56$, not structure. Such combinations are high-degree interpolation polynomials: projector-like elements of the 64-dim algebra.\n- **So:** polynomials in $t_{10},t_{11}$ at $\\theta_1+rh$ **do** localize to site 1 onto all 8 site-1 states, including the singlet. The only low-degree / structured part is $\\{1,\\ t_{11}(\\theta_1),\\ t_{10}(\\theta_1+h)t_{11}(\\theta_1)\\}$.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 1,
   "value": "## Is the singlet-reaching polynomial a mess?\n\nFor comparison, row 8 of the site-1 table in `main-notes.tex`: $Y_8=R(\\theta_1)\\big[t_{21}(\\theta_1)-\\tfrac1{T+1}t_{11}(\\theta_1)R(\\theta_2)\\big]$, with $R=t_{20}t_{10}^{-1}$, $T=\\theta_{12}/h$. Because $\\langle0|$ is cyclic, the algebra element giving a covector is unique. So the degree-4 polynomial in $t(\\theta_1+rh)$ that gives $\\langle0|Y_8$ must equal $Y_8$ as an operator.\n\n(1) Build $Y_8$ and check it. (2) Write it exactly on a 64-monomial basis and look at the coefficient sizes. (3) Search for the sparsest singlet-reaching localized polynomial.",
   "languageId": "markdown",
   "outputs": [],
   "metadata": {}
  },
  {
   "kind": 2,
   "value": "(* companion-frame fused t's at theta_1 and theta_2, same conventions as the SoV table (unnormalised) *)\nct3[u_] := cQM[{1, 2, 3}, {1, 2, 3}][u];\nct20[u_] := ct[{1, 0}][u] . ct[{1, 0}][u + ah] - ct[{1, 1}][u + ah];\nct21[u_] := ct[{1, 1}][u] . ct[{1, 0}][u + ah] - ct3[u + ah];\ncR[u_] := ct20[u] . Inverse[ct[{1, 0}][u]];\nTT = (ath[[1]] - ath[[2]])/ah;\nY8 = cR[ath[[1]]] . (ct21[ath[[1]]] - 1/(TT + 1) ct[{1, 1}][ath[[1]]] . cR[ath[[2]]]);\nv8 = vac0 . Y8;\n{\"Y8 commutes with t's\", And @@ (Y8 . # == # . Y8 & /@ Values[cGen][[{1, 9}]]), \"localized\", isLoc[v8], \"site-1 factor e1 component\", ArrayReshape[v8, {8, 8}][[1, 8]] != 0}",
   "languageId": "wolfram",
   "outputs": [
    {
     "items": [
      {
